<div style="border: 2px solid #1a5276; padding: 15px; text-align: center; background-color: #eaf2f8;">
    <h1 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # ___ – Lesson # 2</h1>
    <p style="color: #1a5276; margin: 5px 0 0 0;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 2</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Reading</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 8th Grade (CEFR: A2.3)</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> Celebrations and Traditions</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> In This Region, They Have a Custom Of...</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Reading: Comprehend descriptions in simple texts. Reading: Summarize the main points of various nonfiction texts. Phonemic Awareness: Demonstrate knowledge of phonemic awareness by pronouncing the irregular present verbs ending in the third person singular "s," "es," and "ies."</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Reading: Can comprehend descriptions in simple texts about cultural traditions (e.g., "What do families do during Fiestas Patrias?" "They decorate their homes with the national flag and participate in the parades."). Can summarize the main points of texts about national festivals by using text features like headings and captions (e.g., "The text highlights key moments of the festival, including parades, dances, and speeches."). Can accurately pronounce irregular present tense verbs ending in the third-person singular "s," "es," and "ies" by producing the correct sounds /s/, /z/, and /ɪz/ in words related to cultural traditions (e.g., /s/ in "decorates," /z/ in "gathers," and /ɪz" in "wishes").</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333; background-color: #eaf2f8;">
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; color: #1a5276; width: 40%;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; color: #1a5276; width: 40%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; color: #1a5276; width: 20%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher displays various images of Panamanian celebrations (e.g., Fiestas Patrias parade, a folk dance, a traditional cuisine dish). Students work in pairs to identify the celebration shown in each picture and recall vocabulary from Lesson 1. The teacher asks, "What do you see in this picture? What custom or tradition does it show?" encouraging use of words like `parade`, `dance`, `celebration`, `traditional`.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher models the pronunciation of key vocabulary words from Lesson 1 (e.g., `celebration`, `parade`, `heritage`, `custom`) emphasizing word stress. The teacher also models the pronunciation of present tense verbs in the third person singular, focusing on the /s/, /z/, and /ɪz/ sounds. For example, pointing to a picture: "Here, she `dances` /s/. The community `gathers` /z/. Everyone `wishes` /ɪz/ for good weather." The teacher writes these verbs on the board, underlining the -s/-es/-ies endings.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b> The teacher checks understanding of the vocabulary and sounds.
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Is a `custom` something new or something people do many times?" (Something people do many times.)</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"When I say 'he `dances`,' does the 's' sound like /s/, /z/, or /ɪz/?" (/s/). "What about 'she `watches`'?" (/ɪz/).</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">10 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 2 — Presentation</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Celebrations and Traditions".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> Students receive a short, simple non-fiction text (150-200 words) titled "Panamanian Fiestas Patrias: A National Custom." The text describes the history and typical activities of Fiestas Patrias, incorporating present simple for descriptions, past simple for historical facts (e.g., "Panama became independent in 1903"), gerunds after prepositions (e.g., "People are excited about celebrating"), and target vocabulary: `tradition`, `custom`, `independence`, `parade`, `celebration`, `dance`, `march`, `national`, `festive`. The text also includes verbs in the third-person singular (e.g., "Panama `celebrates`," "Every family `decorates`," "The crowd `watches`").</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Comprehending descriptions in simple texts about cultural traditions and summarizing main points.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b>
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist questions (First reading):</b> "What is the main topic of the text?" "Is the text about one celebration or many?"</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>True/False (Second reading):</b> Students read statements related to the text and mark them as True or False (e.g., "Panama celebrates Fiestas Patrias in July." "Families decorate their homes with flags.").</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Information matching:</b> Students match headings (e.g., "Historical Fact," "Family Activities," "Parade Traditions") to relevant paragraphs in the text.</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">10 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Reading.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Reading comprehension for specific details, identifying main points, and practicing pronunciation of third-person singular verbs in context. Reinforce present simple, past simple, gerunds after prepositions, and target vocabulary (`tradition`, `custom`, `parade`, `celebration`, `dance`, `gather`, `enjoy`, `march`, `traditional`, `cultural`, `national`, `festive`).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b>
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">1. <b>Controlled practice (Cloze/Gap-fill):</b> Students re-read the text, and complete sentences with missing vocabulary words (e.g., "The Fiestas Patrias is an important national _______.") or the correct form of present simple verbs, paying attention to third-person singular (e.g., "Every November, Panama (celebrate) _______ its independence."). The teacher monitors, providing support for vocabulary and verb forms.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">2. <b>Semi-controlled (Sentence completion):</b> Students answer specific detail questions based on the text in complete sentences. For example: "What do people do during the parades?" (They `march` and `dance` in the parades.) "When did Panama become independent?" (Panama `became` independent in 1903.) "What are families excited about doing for Fiestas Patrias?" (Families are excited about `celebrating` with their relatives.)</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">3. <b>Pair Reading & Pronunciation Check:</b> In pairs, students take turns reading aloud specific sentences from the text that contain verbs ending in -s, -es, or -ies (e.g., "The whole community `gathers` in the main square." "A musician `plays` traditional drums."). Their partner listens carefully and gives feedback on the pronunciation of the /s/, /z/, or /ɪz/ sounds. The teacher circulates, modeling correct pronunciation as needed.</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">15 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Celebrations and Traditions".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Collaborative Summary and Mini-Presentation.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Students work in small groups (3-4 students). Each group re-reads the "Panamanian Fiestas Patrias" text. Their task is to summarize the main points of the text, highlighting what the festival includes (e.g., "The text highlights key moments of the festival, including parades, dances, and speeches."). They must identify three key traditions or customs described in the text. They prepare a brief oral summary to share with the class (1-2 minutes per group). They are encouraged to use present simple to describe traditions and incorporate target vocabulary words. During their mini-presentation, they should pay attention to pronouncing the third-person singular verbs correctly.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to Theme:</b> After each group presents, the teacher facilitates a brief class discussion asking questions like: "What traditions are important in your community, similar to Fiestas Patrias?" or "My favorite tradition is the parade because..." (prompting expression of preferences).</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">10 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist during group discussions and presentations, focusing on reading comprehension, summarization skills, and pronunciation of target verbs. Peer assessment can also be incorporated where groups rate each other's summaries based on clarity and detail.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students complete an Exit Ticket. They receive a new, very short paragraph (3-4 sentences) about a different Panamanian custom (e.g., Carnival masks). They read the text silently and then:
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">1. Write one main idea from the paragraph.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">2. Identify two verbs in the third-person singular form.</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b>
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Ability to comprehend the main idea of a simple text.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Accuracy in identifying third-person singular verbs.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Appropriate use of target vocabulary in summaries (from Stage 4).</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Clarity and conciseness of the summary (from Stage 4).</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b>
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was the most interesting custom you read about today?"</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What is one thing you learned about summarizing texts today?"</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">7 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 6 — Reflection</b></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection:</b> Students reflect on their learning by responding to the following questions in their notebooks or orally:
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What did you find easy or difficult about understanding and summarizing the texts about traditions today?"</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How can reading about different traditions help you appreciate your own and other cultures?"</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b>
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Which reading activities were most effective in helping students comprehend descriptions and summarize main points? Did students successfully identify key details and demonstrate understanding of the third-person singular verb forms?"</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What adjustments or additional scaffolding might be needed for students who struggled with summarization or pronunciation?"</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Was the text appropriately challenging for A2.3 learners, and did it adequately incorporate the target grammar and vocabulary?"</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson in the theme sequence:</b> "Today we read about traditions and practiced our reading skills. Next lesson, we will use these descriptions and our knowledge of celebrations to practice speaking and interacting with each other about our favorite customs and what people do during these festivities."</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">3 min</td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>